{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310726,"nonce":"thGcaNGPN39IOY/5Ik5TCR+XooY=","title":"FIXED 2026-06-06 (commit 290e475). Cross-block effectful-Call egraph panic that surfaced as Sys.getEnv-in-ctor SIGILL. Two-part fix: (1) ...","message":"**RESOLVED at commit 290e475** — two-part fix in hir_to_mir.rs + scalar_replacement.rs.\n\n\n**hir_to_mir.rs**: For each effectful-Call result reg defined in the\ncond-eval block that is also bound to a symbol_map entry, emit a\nsingle-incoming phi at the entry of `then_block` and `else_block`\nwith incoming `(cond_eval_block, reg)`, then rebind\n`symbol_map[sym]` to that phi while lowering each branch. Routes\nthe cross-block use through a Cranelift block-arg/phi instead of a\ndirect SSA reference. Applied in both `lower_conditional_typed`\n(if-as-expression) and `lower_if_statement` (if-as-statement).\n\n**scalar_replacement.rs `apply_sra`**: Rewrote field-tracking from\na single global `field_current` Vec to proper per-block\n`field_in`/`field_out` reaching-definitions with phi insertion at\nmulti-pred merge blocks. The old single global tracker overwrote\nbb_then's field value with bb_else's value in BFS order, so the\nmerge-block load only saw the LAST branch's value. After inlining,\nthis manifested as one branch having no defined value for the\nSROA'd field at the merge — invalid SSA, different egraph panic.\nThe branch-phi fix could not work end-to-end until SROA itself was\nSSA-correct.\n\n**scalar_replacement.rs `find_phi_sra_candidates`**: Skip\nsingle-incoming \"branch-bridge\" phis emitted by the new lowering.\nThey look like phi-SRA candidates (incoming is a malloc result) but\nthey're not multi-allocation merges and shouldn't be flattened.\n\n\n- `/tmp/min2.hx` (Sys.getEnv in ctor + if-store): exit=132 → exit=0\n- `/tmp/min_ctor.hx` with FOO unset → \"fallback\"; with FOO=hello → \"hello\"\n- 128/128 haxe regression PASS\n- llama-chat short-form: 21.43 tok/s median (vs 21.34 baseline, within noise)\n- cargo clippy on modified files: clean\n- agent worktree also verified ternary form + else-less if + nue tiny-transformer\n\n\n- Future Sys.getEnv-in-ctor patterns can be written directly without\n  the \"read in a method, thread bool through ctor\" workaround\n- The Q8 KV cache attempt's `Sys.getEnv` blocker (per\n  [[bugs-q8-kv-cache-attempted]]) is now unblocked\n- The naive \"use `if (env != null) { x = env; } else { x = \"default\" }`\n  in any constructor\" pattern compiles correctly\n- The `bugs_q8_kv_cache_attempted.md` plan to use \"opaque extern\n  class wrapping the cache; read env in Main + thread through ctor\"\n  is no longer needed as a workaround\n\n\nThe pre-fix diagnosis below stays for reference — it traced four\nfailed bridge attempts (Copy, BinOp Add 0, stack-slot store/load,\nplain branch-phi alone) before the agent identified that the\nremaining issue was a SROA correctness bug, not an inliner bug. The\nagent's diagnosis showed the \"invalid SSA on then-path\" symptom was\nSROA overwriting field values in BFS order across `bb_then` and\n`bb_else` — a latent bug in SROA that pre-dated the egraph panic\nand was masked by the panic firing first.\n\n---\n\n\n**Proximate cause IDENTIFIED but NOT fixed** — Cranelift egraph elaboration panic.\n\n\nThe bug is NOT constructor-specific. It triggers in any function (ctor, method, OR `main`) with this MIR pattern:\n\n```\nblock0:\n    vN = call @effectful_extern   ; e.g. haxe_sys_get_env\n    ...\n    brif cond, block1, block2\n\nblock1:\n    store field, vN              ; cross-block use of effectful Call result\n    jump merge\nblock2:\n    vM = call @other_effectful   ; e.g. haxe_string_literal(\"fallback\")\n    store field, vM\n    jump merge\n```\n\nPanics in `cranelift/codegen/src/egraph/elaborate.rs:722` with `\"elaborating effectful instructions, should have remained in the skeleton\"` when `OptimizationTier::Standard.cranelift_opt_level() = \"speed\"` triggers egraph.\n\nMinimal repro `/tmp/min2.hx`:\n```haxe\nclass Wrapper { public var got:String; public function new() {\n  var v = Sys.getEnv(\"FOO\");\n  if (v != null) { got = v; } else { got = \"fallback\"; }\n}}\nclass Main { public static function main() { var w = new Wrapper(); Sys.println(w.got); } }\n```\n\nWorking workaround `/tmp/min5.hx`: write to a LOCAL in both branches, then a single field-store after merge. Forces a phi-merge through a local, sidestepping the cross-block effectful use.\n\n\n1. **18afed8** (`hir_to_mir: ctor body inherits the function-body lowering setup`) — closed four real structural deltas in `lower_constructor_body` but didn't address this bug class. The fix is correct + safe; just unrelated.\n\n2. **Bridge fix attempt #1 (MIR Copy)** 2026-06-06: emit `Copy { dest: bridge, src: call_dest }` in `lower_conditional_typed` before the branch, plus guard `CopyPropagationPass` to skip propagating Copies whose `src` is an effectful Call dest. The bridge fires (verified via debug print) and survives copy-prop, but **Cranelift's MIR→CLIF lowering elides Copy entirely** — `IrInstruction::Copy` just aliases `dest` to `src` in `value_map` (cranelift_backend.rs:2426-2433). No CLIF instruction emitted. Both `dest` and `src` resolve to the same Cranelift SSA value.\n\n3. **Bridge fix attempt #2 (BinOp Add 0)** 2026-06-06: emit `BinOp Add(src, const 0)` instead of Copy. This DOES produce a real `iadd v10, v11` instruction in CLIF (verified via `RAYZOR_DUMP_CLIF`). But Cranelift's egraph optimization folds `x + 0 = x` during e-class union, so it still sees through the bridge to the underlying effectful Call result. Panic unchanged.\n\n4. **Bridge fix attempt #3 (stack-slot store/load)** 2026-06-06: alloca a stack slot, store src to it, load it back, use the load result as the bridge. CLIF dump confirms the slot + store + load all survive to codegen. But **Cranelift's egraph alias-analyzes through memory operations** — it forwards `store slot, x` → `load slot` as `x` during elaboration. Panic unchanged.\n\n\nComparing CLIFs of broken `/tmp/min2.hx` vs working `/tmp/min5.hx` (workaround shape) reveals that **min5 routes the bridged value through Cranelift block-args** (i.e. phi nodes via `jump block3(v12)`), while min2 uses it via direct cross-block SSA reference. Both have the same stack_addr/store/load instructions in entry — the difference is whether the BRANCH BODY uses the value via a block-arg or directly.\n\nSo the real fix is restructuring `lower_conditional_typed` so the bridged value is passed to each branch via a block param (phi node at the start of each branch block) rather than referenced directly via the entry block's symbol_map entry.\n\n\nImplemented exactly this: in `lower_conditional_typed`, before `build_cond_branch`, identify call-result locals; emit `build_phi(then_block, ty)` + `add_phi_incoming(then_block, phi, cond_eval_block, src_reg)` for each, then similarly for `else_block`. After `switch_to_block(then_block)`, rebind `symbol_map[sym] = then_phi`. Same for else.\n\n**Standalone result: SUCCESS.** Wrapper.new's CLIF now shows the correct Cranelift block-arg routing:\n```\nblock0: ... v10 = call fn3(v9) ... brif v13, block1(v10), block2\nblock1(v22: i64):       ; v22 is the block param (= my phi)\n    store v22, ... ; uses block-arg, not v10\n```\nSame shape as min5's working CLIF.\n\n**Inlined result: FAILS.** When `Main.main` calls `new Wrapper()`, the inliner copies Wrapper.new's blocks into main and SROA tries to fold the field-set + field-read pattern. The resulting CLIF for main has:\n```\nblock2: jump block4               ; \"then\" branch — NO STORE, no value defined\nblock3: v16 = call fn5(...); ...  ; \"else\" branch defines v16\nblock5: ... call fn7(v16) ...     ; uses v16 but it's undefined on the then-path\n```\nInvalid SSA on the then-path. The Cranelift egraph panic still fires, but for a different reason now: undefined value on a control-flow path.\n\nThis means there's a **second bug in the inliner / scalar replacement** that the egraph-bridge fix alone can't address. When inlining a function with branch-phi'd Call results, SROA folds the immediate `store-field; load-field` into direct value passing but loses one branch's value.\n\n\nThe branch-phi fix in `lower_conditional_typed` is the right structural pattern for the standalone case. The remaining work is to also fix the inliner's phi/SROA interaction so that the inlined version of a fixed function preserves the value flow. That's another 1-2 session investigation focused on `compiler/src/ir/inlining.rs` and `compiler/src/ir/scalar_replacement.rs`.\n\nFor now: revert all attempts (tree is clean at 18afed8 + a241cc9 + 655d7ac), keep the workaround (read env in a method, thread the bool through ctor args).\n\n\nEach is substantially more invasive than the original scope:\n\n1. **Modify Cranelift `Copy` lowering** (cranelift_backend.rs:2426) to emit a real CLIF instruction (e.g. `iadd_imm src, 0`) when `src` is the dest of an effectful CallDirect. Creates a true SSA boundary. Risk: penalises all `Copy` instructions; needs care to only apply to the bridge case.\n\n2. **Introduce a new MIR instruction kind** `IrInstruction::SsaBarrier { dest, src }` that always lowers to a real CLIF instruction. Use it in `lower_conditional_typed` instead of `Copy`. Cleaner than (1) but requires touching every backend (Cranelift, LLVM, C, interpreter).\n\n3. **Hoist field-store-in-branch in MIR**: in `lower_conditional_typed`, detect when both branches end with a field store to the same field and rewrite to local-store-then-merge-then-field-store. Mirrors the min5 workaround at MIR level. Risk: complex pattern matching; may miss cases.\n\n(2) is probably the right one — cleanest, most semantically meaningful. Estimated 1-2 sessions of focused work.\n\n\nRead `Sys.getEnv` in `nue.Main` or `LlamaArch.build` (a METHOD, not a class ctor body with `if (v != null) { field = v; }`) and thread the resulting value through ctor args. Currently used by `lm_head` re-quant gate at `LlamaArch.hx:124` (`var optOut = Sys.getEnv(\"RAYZOR_REQUANT_LM_HEAD\")`).\n\n\nThe \"Sys.getEnv-in-ctor\" name oversells the constructor angle. The bug fires in any function with the cross-block effectful-Call-result + if/else-with-field-store pattern. Calling it \"egraph cross-block effectful use\" would be more accurate.\n\n\nUser asked: \"doesn't the init call register all global constants ahead of calling main? I believe we have a global table\" — implying the bug stemmed from the global table being populated AFTER constructors run.\n\n**Refuted by code reading.** Cranelift's `JITBuilder::with_symbols()` (cranelift_backend.rs:275-290) registers runtime extern pointers at backend construction time, BEFORE any function compiles. `__init__` / `__vtable_init__` (hir_to_mir.rs:36646-36703, 36438-36623) populate Haxe globals BEFORE any `execute_function()` call (including `main`). By the time any ctor body executes, both the runtime symbol table and the Haxe global init have already run. The bug is NOT racing init.\n\n\n```\nexit=132   # SIGILL\n[gen] streaming...   # last line before crash\n```\n\nAdding `var v = Sys.getEnv(\"X\")` to a Haxe class's `new()` body — even once, even for a class instantiated exactly once during model build — causes the program to SIGILL midway through decode setup. Removing the Sys.getEnv call (hardcoding the boolean) makes the program work normally.\n\nTriggered during a fused-RoPE-LUT attempt this session at the next-iter perf work. RoPE is constructed ONCE in `LlamaArch.build`, so it's NOT the 16-layer multi-ctor pattern documented in `bugs_q8_kv_cache_attempted.md`. Even a single Sys.getEnv-in-ctor is enough.\n\n\n`655d7ac` (inliner SSA counter sync) fixed the duplicate-SSA-def class of bugs that GVN was aliasing into trap-stub installs. This bug has a different signature — it crashes with SIGILL at runtime rather than getting trap-stubbed at compile time. The two bug surfaces overlap in symptoms (W0020 messages can also lead to SIGILLs) but the underlying mechanism is distinct.\n\nThe previous memory `bugs_q8_kv_cache_attempted.md` attributed the same symptom to \"new fields shifted class layout, surfacing latent JIT bugs in large import graphs\" — but the field-shift framing turned out to be wrong (resolved at 655d7ac). The Sys.getEnv-in-ctor part is a SEPARATE residual bug.\n\n\nRead `Sys.getEnv` in a method that runs once at startup — `nue.Main.main` and `nue.arch.LlamaArch.build` are both known-good — and thread the resulting bool through the chain into the affected class's constructor as an explicit argument.\n\nExample (used for the fused-RoPE attempt before it washed out perf-wise):\n\n```haxe\n// In LlamaArch.build (a METHOD, not a constructor):\nvar ropeFusedEnv = Sys.getEnv(\"RAYZOR_ROPE_FUSED\");\nvar useFusedRope = (ropeFusedEnv != null \u0026\u0026 ropeFusedEnv != \"0\" \u0026\u0026 ropeFusedEnv != \"\");\nvar rope = new RoPE(meta.headDim, meta.maxSeqLen, meta.ropeBase, useFusedRope);\n\n// In RoPE.new (the constructor — NO Sys.getEnv here):\npublic function new(headDim:Int, maxSeqLen:Int, base:Float = 10000.0, useFused:Bool = false) {\n    this.useFused = useFused;  // already-decided bool, threaded in\n    ...\n}\n```\n\n\nAny Haxe class with a `Sys.getEnv` in its constructor body, instantiated from nue/llama-chat (or any program that load+decodes a Llama model), reproduces. The crash is BEFORE generation completes — typically at `[gen] streaming...` last line.\n\n\nNeeds a compiler investigation. The plausible mechanism is the `haxe_sys_get_env` extern's argument-marshalling or return-type-handling differing between method-call lowering and ctor-call lowering. The `runtime_mapping` entry for `Sys.getEnv` should be inspected, and the MIR-level call shape compared in the two contexts.\n\nThis bug has been seen at least twice now (Q8 KV cache attempt 2026-06-05, RoPE fused attempt 2026-06-06) and both times was worked around rather than fixed. Worth a focused session.\n\n\n- [[bugs-q8-kv-cache-attempted]] — first time we hit this. Previous memory attributed it to field-shift; this update clarifies the actual mechanism.\n- [[bugs-w0020-value-map-miss-cross-loop]] — RESOLVED at 655d7ac. Different bug class — that one was SSA counter desync triggering GVN aliasing. This residual bug is independent.\n- [[feedback-no-silent-dispatch-fallthrough]] — same principle: when a hidden constraint trips at codegen, ERROR loudly; don't install a SIGILL'ing function silently.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_sys_getenv_in_ctor_residual` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}